<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Tag Spotter — App Privacy Manifest

  Required by App Store review. Two things are declared here, and both are
  deliberately narrow:

  1. NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes — "required reason" APIs.

     @capacitor/preferences stores the save file in UserDefaults and ships no
     privacy manifest of its own (Capacitor core ships one, but it is empty), so
     the declaration has to live at the app level or review will flag it.
     Reason CA92.1 = access only to values written by this app itself, which is
     exactly what the save file is.

     Nothing else is declared, because nothing else is used. Capacitor's own
     manifest asserts no required-reason APIs, and Geolocation, Haptics,
     Keyboard, StatusBar and App do not touch the file-timestamp, disk-space or
     active-keyboard categories. Over-declaring is not free: it invites questions
     about APIs the app never calls.

  2. NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes — deliberately EMPTY.

     Apple defines "collect" as transmitting data off the device. Tag Spotter
     reads a single coarse location when a plate is marked as found, converts it
     to a distance bonus, and discards it — the coordinates never enter app state
     and there is no backend to send them to (see docs/privacy-policy.md and
     dec-0009 on plain-JSON local saves). Location is *used*, not *collected*.

     If a leaderboard or any sync is ever added, this array stops being empty and
     the Play Data Safety answers change with it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
